{"t":1792095635,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"ab5cfe18-e704-4f11-84fc-4251b1a62554","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-15T19:15:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 151915\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n315 PM EDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\n\nSmall Craft Advisories and a subsequent Gale Watch have been issued \nfor increasing winds starting Friday afternoon through Sunday \nmorning. Sky & PoPs outlooks were slightly changed to reflect \nongoing precipitation. \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) A strong cold front brings gusty winds & potential for severe \nthunderstorms on Friday.\n\n\n- 2) Below average temperatures Sunday through Wednesday.\n  Possible frost and freezing temperatures in the Appalachians\n  early Sunday. Dry each period.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A strong cold front brings gusty winds & potential for severe \nthunderstorms on Friday.\n\nAs a passing cold front sags to the south, scattered showers will \ncontinue to move through the region throughout this afternoon and \ninto tonight. Instability remains limited, limiting thunderstorm \ncoverage overall as this front gradually slides south of the area \novernight. \n\nBy tomorrow, a brief period of warm temperatures and sunny \nconditions returns as southwest winds gradually increase throughout \nthe day. Meanwhile, a deep digging trough forming over the Midwest \nwill quickly traverse the Ohio River Valley before tracking towards \nthe East Coast, forming a line of thunderstorms that will move \nthrough in the afternoon & evening timeframe. Combined with warmer \ntemperatures (with highs in the 80s by Friday afternoon) and low-end \ninstability, some of these thunderstorms could become severe as the \nassociated cold front tracks through the region. Due to conditions \nremaining relatively dry (ensemble guidance indicates PWATs ranging \nfrom 1.2-1.4 inches), the primary hazard will likely be gusty to \ndamaging winds as DCAPE ranges in the low-1000s and shear remains \nelevated. SPC has included the metro regions in a Slight risk for \nsevere weather impacts during this time period. While damaging wind \ngusts are going to be the primary threat, a quick QLCS tornado spin-\nup is certainly possible. \n\nThis line of thunderstorms pushes east of the area by late Friday \nnight, but the trough will lag behind this line of thunderstorms and \ntraverse the area throughout the day Saturday. Thus, additional rain \nshowers remain possible into the weekend as this trough finally \npushes through. While winds likely remain elevated through this \nperiod, with peak gusts over the mountains approaching 30-40 MPH, \nmost areas likely stay below wind advisory criteria. Conditions \nbegin to clear by Sunday, with much colder temperatures settling in \nin the wake of the front. Highs on Saturday only reach the\nmid-50s as the trough moves through, with continued cooler\ntemperatures anticipated through the start of next week. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below average temperatures Sunday through Wednesday. Possible \nfrost and freezing temperatures in the Appalachians early Sunday. \nDry each period.\n\nA sharp transition to cold weather is expected behind a strong cold \nfront this weekend. This dry airmass in combination with clearing \nskies and weakening winds creates an ideal environment for \nefficient radiational cooling conditions throughout the area. \nOvernight temperatures could drop to near or below freezing west\nof the Blue Ridge on Saturday and Sunday night. Most areas \nacross our region have opportunities for frost to form, \nparticularly west of I-95.\n\nHigh pressure will build across the region Sunday through Wednesday.\nLight and variable winds in the mountains of the Appalachians could \nresult in areas of frost and near freezing temperatures Sunday \nmorning. Elsewhere and otherwise, dry conditions Sunday through \nWednesday in part to the area of high pressure. Temperatures will \nmodify some toward late Tuesday into Wednesday.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /19Z THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\n\nTerminals will continue to see gradually lowering CIGs today as a \nstalling cold front produces scattered showers throughout the \nafternoon & evening. Periods of IFR are likely this evening into \ntonight, with CIGs gradually improving by Friday morning. Some \npatchy fog could linger in the early morning after showers \ntransition east, with VFR conditions returning soon afterwards. \n\nA second cold front bringing scattered showers and thunderstorms \nwill bring back sub-VFR conditions beginning Friday afternoon and \ninto the evening. Some of these storms could become severe, \nproducing gusty to damaging winds. Low clouds from this line of \nthunderstorms could linger into Saturday morning, with conditions \nreturning to VFR after sunrise Saturday. \n\nEasterly winds shift southerly by Friday morning, with wind gusts up \nto 20-25 knots possible ahead of the cold front. Winds shift \nnorthwesterly by Saturday morning in the wake of the front, with \ngusts staying elevated throughout the day Saturday before \ndiminishing overnight. \n\nVFR conditions Sunday through Monday night. Winds north 5 to 15 \nknots Sunday, becoming light and variable Sunday night through \nMonday night.\n\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\n\nWinds have fallen below SCA criteria as of this afternoon. Easterly \nwinds shift southerly overnight, staying below SCA thresholds until \nFriday morning. Winds could gust up to 20-25 knots Friday afternoon, \nwith locally higher gusts possible throughout the afternoon & \nevening as scattered thunderstorms move through the region. Winds \nshift northwesterly early Saturday morning in the wake of the cold \nfront, potentially reaching gale conditions later that day. A Gale \nWatch has been issued for the lower portions of the Bay until Sunday \nmorning as this front pushes through.  \n\nWinds will diminish Sunday afternoon through Monday night. Winds \nnortheast 15 to 20 knots gusts 25 to 30 knots Sunday morning. Winds \nquickly diminishing 10 to 15 knots Sunday afternoon through Monday \nnight and becoming light and variable.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Small Craft Advisory from 9 AM Friday to 6 AM EDT Saturday for \n     MDZ008.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory from 9 AM Friday to 6 AM EDT Saturday for \n     ANZ530>543.\n     Gale Watch from Saturday morning through late Saturday night \n     for ANZ532>534-537-543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...SRT/KLW/KRR\nAVIATION...SRT/KLW\nMARINE...SRT/KLW\n"},"forecast":{"t":1792095584,"days":{"2026-10-15":{"hi":67,"lo":59,"pop":49,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-10-16":{"hi":83,"lo":50,"pop":78,"short":"Patchy Fog then Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-17":{"hi":55,"lo":38,"pop":21,"short":"Slight Chance Rain Showers then Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-18":{"hi":58,"lo":42,"pop":6,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-19":{"hi":63,"lo":45,"pop":7,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-20":{"hi":67,"lo":49,"pop":5,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-21":{"hi":68,"lo":48,"pop":5,"short":"Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1792094400,"n":48,"ceil":[4900,4700,4400,3900,3600,2800,2800,2400,2200,1700,900,700,600,500,400,400,400,400,500,1100,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,5100,5300,5600,5800,6200,6500,6900,5700,4400,3200,3500,3700,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,9.9,9.8,7.8,7.0,7.0,7.0,5.0,4.3,3.4,3.3,3.9,7.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,4.0,1.0,1.0,4.0,10.0,5.0,3.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0],"spd":[5,4,5,5,5,4,4,4,4,3,3,3,4,4,5,5,6,7,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,10,10,9,8,8,7,8,7,7,8,9,10,11,12,13,13,13,13,13,13,13,13],"gst":[7,8,8,9,10,10,10,10,9,9,9,9,10,11,11,12,13,14,15,15,14,15,15,16,16,16,17,18,17,17,16,16,16,16,15,16,17,17,19,20,21,21,20,20,20,21,21,21],"dir":[70,90,90,1